{
  "severity": "high",
  "network_observations": [
    {"ip": "10.30.2.10", "inferred_role": "domain_controller", "first_seen": "2028-02-14T01:23:18+0100", "last_seen": "2028-02-14T01:33:18+0100", "agent_name": "DC-CORP-01"},
    {"ip": "10.30.7.84", "inferred_role": "anomalous_rdp_source_for_svc_account", "first_seen": "2028-02-14T01:23:18+0100", "last_seen": "2028-02-14T01:23:18+0100"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "Lateral Movement / Valid Accounts", "technique": "T1078.002 + T1021.001", "what_happened": "At 2028-02-14 01:23:18 CET, Windows Security Event 4624 fired on DC-CORP-01 (10.30.2.10): TargetUserName=svc_backup, logonType=10 (RemoteInteractive = RDP), IpAddress=10.30.7.84, AuthenticationPackageName=Kerberos. svc_backup is a service account with Domain Admin equivalent rights — service accounts NEVER perform interactive RDP logons under normal operations (they authenticate via logonType=4 batch or logonType=5 service). A service account doing RDP TO a Domain Controller from a workstation source IP is the unambiguous compromised-svc-account-pivoting-to-Tier-0 signature. The most plausible scenarios: (a) svc_backup credentials were extracted from a memory dump / unattend.xml / GPP cpassword on a previously-compromised host and replayed by an attacker, (b) svc_backup TGT was harvested via Pass-the-Ticket and used to authenticate via RDP. Either way, an attacker with Domain Admin equivalent rights is now interactively sitting on the DC.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 4624 (data.win.system.eventID=4624) on agent.name=DC-CORP-01, targetUserName=svc_backup, logonType=10, ipAddress=10.30.7.84. The combination of (service account name pattern svc_*) + (logonType=10 RDP) + (target=Domain Controller) is the canonical Tier-0-pivot signature.", "first_seen": "2028-02-14T01:23:18+0100"},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Persistence (Defense Evasion via GPO)", "technique": "T1484.001", "what_happened": "At 01:32:18 CET (9 minutes after the interactive logon), Sysmon Event 11 (FileCreate) fired on DC-CORP-01: targetFilename=\\\\filesrv\\sysvol\\corp.local\\Policies\\{31B2F340-016D-11D2-945F-00C04FB984F9}\\Machine\\Scripts\\Startup\\update.ps1. The GUID {31B2F340-016D-11D2-945F-00C04FB984F9} is the well-known Default Domain Policy — the GPO linked at domain root and applied to EVERY domain machine on every gpupdate refresh cycle. The file dropped is a PowerShell script (.ps1), signed=false, SHA256=b1a2c3d4e5f6071829304a5b6c7d8e9f0a1b2c3d4e5f6071829304a5b6c7d8e9. The Machine\\Scripts\\Startup path is where Windows reads STARTUP SCRIPTS — they execute as NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM on every domain machine at boot. This is domain-wide persistence: ONE file drop affects every machine in corp.local.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 11 on agent.name=DC-CORP-01, targetFilename containing the Default Domain Policy GPO GUID path + Machine\\Scripts\\Startup subdirectory + .ps1 extension. The combination of (Default Domain Policy GUID) + (Machine\\Scripts\\Startup) + (unsigned .ps1) is the unambiguous GPO-startup-script-persistence signature (MITRE T1484.001 Domain Policy Modification — GPO Modification).", "first_seen": "2028-02-14T01:32:18+0100"},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "Persistence (AD Modification — Smoking Gun)", "technique": "T1484.001", "what_happened": "At 01:32:44 CET, Windows Security Event 5136 'A directory service object was modified' fired on DC-CORP-01: ObjectDN=CN={31B2F340-016D-11D2-945F-00C04FB984F9},CN=Policies,CN=System,DC=corp,DC=local (the Default Domain Policy GPO container in AD), AttributeLDAPDisplayName=gPCMachineExtensionNames, OperationType=Value Added, SubjectUserName=svc_backup. The attribute gPCMachineExtensionNames is the AD-side registration that tells gpupdate WHICH client-side extension (CSE) GUIDs to apply for a GPO — modifying it = activating the new startup script extension. This Event 5136 is the SMOKING GUN of GPO modification at the AD layer; the file drop (step 2) alone would not take effect without this AD-side attribute write. The subject = svc_backup confirms the attacker's privilege was used to make the modification.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 5136 on agent.name=DC-CORP-01, objectDN containing the Default Domain Policy GUID + CN=Policies,CN=System, attributeLDAPDisplayName=gPCMachineExtensionNames, operationType='Value Added', subjectUserName=svc_backup. The combination of (5136) + (Default Domain Policy GPO container) + (gPCMachineExtensionNames attribute write) + (Value Added) is the explicit GPO-modification-via-LDAP signature.", "first_seen": "2028-02-14T01:32:44+0100"},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Persistence (AD Object Creation)", "technique": "T1484.001", "what_happened": "At 01:33:01 CET (17 seconds after the 5136), Windows Security Event 5137 'A directory service object was created' fired on DC-CORP-01: ObjectDN within the Default Domain Policy GPO subtree, related to the startup script extension class registration. Event 5137 typically accompanies 5136 for GPO extension wiring — when the gPCMachineExtensionNames attribute lists a new CSE GUID, the corresponding container object must exist in the GPO subtree to hold the extension data. The 5136-then-5137 17-second pair is the canonical GPO-modification-with-new-CSE workflow.", "chain_reason": "Windows Security Event 5137 on agent.name=DC-CORP-01, objectDN within the Default Domain Policy GPO subtree, temporally aligned (17s) with the 5136 attribute write — confirms GPO extension wiring as a unit.", "first_seen": "2028-02-14T01:33:01+0100"},
    {"step": 5, "tactic": "Persistence (SYSVOL Replication Registration)", "technique": "T1484.001", "what_happened": "At 01:33:18 CET, SYSVOL replication audit log shows \\\\filesrv\\sysvol\\corp.local\\Policies\\{31B2F340-016D-11D2-945F-00C04FB984F9}\\Machine\\Scripts\\scripts.ini was updated. The scripts.ini file is the INI-format manifest that Windows reads at boot to know which startup scripts to execute and in what order. Registering update.ps1 in scripts.ini completes the persistence: now any domain machine, on next gpupdate refresh + boot, will (a) sync the new GPO version via SYSVOL DFSR replication, (b) read scripts.ini, (c) execute update.ps1 as SYSTEM via gpscript.exe. The persistence is now FULLY ARMED.", "chain_reason": "SYSVOL replication audit log entry for scripts.ini under the Default Domain Policy Machine\\Scripts path. The (scripts.ini update) + (referencing update.ps1) + (within the Default Domain Policy GPO) is the explicit startup-script-manifest-registration signature.", "first_seen": "2028-02-14T01:33:18+0100"},
    {"step": 6, "tactic": "Execution (Pending — Next gpupdate / Reboot Cycle)", "technique": "T1059.001 + T1037.003", "what_happened": "At the next GPO refresh interval (default 90 minutes ±30 randomization) or next reboot on any domain machine, Sysmon Event 1 will fire fleet-wide: image=powershell.exe + commandLine='powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File \\\\filesrv\\sysvol\\corp.local\\Policies\\{31B2F340-016D-11D2-945F-00C04FB984F9}\\Machine\\Scripts\\Startup\\update.ps1' + parentImage=C:\\Windows\\System32\\gpscript.exe + user=NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM. The parent=gpscript.exe is the smoking gun: gpscript.exe is the Windows Group Policy scripts engine that runs ONLY startup/shutdown/logon/logoff scripts. SYSTEM-context execution means the attacker code has unrestricted access on every host — credential dump, ransomware deployment, additional persistence — anything. Hosts that have already executed this script as of investigation time = already compromised at SYSTEM level; this is the Q4 forward-hunt question.", "chain_reason": "Sysmon Event 1 with image=powershell.exe + parentImage=gpscript.exe + user='NT AUTHORITY\\\\SYSTEM' + commandLine containing the SYSVOL Default Domain Policy startup script path. The parent=gpscript.exe is decisive — no other Windows binary launches PowerShell as SYSTEM at boot via a SYSVOL UNC path.", "first_seen": "2028-02-14T01:33:18+0100"}
  ],
